In a message dated 10/5/2002 6:28:09 AM Central Daylight Time, a.rosta@lycos.co.uk writes:



<<

My own view is that 3-valued truth functions applies to a simple

proposition is very useful, but applied to other truth functions

recursively is mind-bogglingly complicated and not useful in

practice.



Of the 27 possible unary operators for 3-value logic, I'm in not

in favour of those that output a 0 (e.g. {+,0,0}), except for

{+,0,-} and {-,0,+} (varieties of NA and JA'A).

>>

Painfully true, but not so bad if you settle for a small set that represent in some useful fashion the main connectives you want (and that have easy patterns to remember: Min, Max, Mirror and so on).  This can then go pretty easily to any number of values you want -- even reals (it is Sum and Product tht get you).  



